findings.body:
`UpdateCommandDistTest.testFeatureNotEnabled` (line 47) is annotated `@Launch({UpdateCompatibility.NAME, UpdateCompatibilityMetadata.NAME})` — it exercises the feature-disabled guard **only** for the `metadata` subcommand. The `check` subcommand (`UpdateCompatibilityCheck`) has an identical guard added in this PR (lines 45-49 of `UpdateCompatibilityCheck.java`):

```java
if (!Profile.isFeatureEnabled(Profile.Feature.ROLLING_UPDATES)) {
    printFeatureDisabled();
    picocli.exit(CompatibilityResult.FEATURE_DISABLED);
    return;
}
```

But there is no `@Launch({UpdateCompatibility.NAME, UpdateCompatibilityCheck.NAME})` test without `ENABLE_FEATURE` to verify the check subcommand prints the disabled message and exits with code 4 when the feature is off. The two guards are independent code paths in independent `@CommandLine.Command` classes; the metadata test does not transitively cover the check command. A regression that breaks only the check guard (e.g., someone removes the guard from `UpdateCompatibilityCheck` but not `UpdateCompatibilityMetadata`) would pass CI silently.

findings.suggestion:
Add a second test method, or parameterize the existing one, to also launch `{UpdateCompatibility.NAME, UpdateCompatibilityCheck.NAME}` without `--features=rolling-updates` and assert the same error message (and ideally exit code 4). Example:
```java
@Test
@Launch({UpdateCompatibility.NAME, UpdateCompatibilityCheck.NAME})
public void testFeatureNotEnabledCheck(CLIResult cliResult) {
    cliResult.assertError("Unable to use this command. The preview feature 'rolling-updates' is not enabled.");
}
```

findings.body:
The added comment block:
```java
// see picocli.CommandLine.ExitCode
// 1 -> software error
// 2 -> usage error
int RECREATE_UPGRADE_EXIT_CODE = 3;
int FEATURE_DISABLED = 4;
```
explains only why `1` and `2` were avoided. It does **not** explain why `3` was chosen for recreate-upgrade and `4` for feature-disabled, nor the historical fact that recreate-upgrade was previously `4` and was renumbered. Since the `RECREATE_UPGRADE_EXIT_CODE` value is a public scripting contract, the Javadoc on the interface (which already says _"to help building scripts around this tool"_) should document the full exit-code table and flag the renumbering as a breaking change for consumers migrating from the previous `4` value.

Additionally, the comment is placed as line comments (`//`) rather than Javadoc (`/** */`), so it does not surface in generated API docs — yet these constants are `public` and part of the public API.

The exact same 4-line guard is copy-pasted at the top of both `UpdateCompatibilityCheck.run()` (lines 45-49) and `UpdateCompatibilityMetadata.run()` (lines 45-49):

```java
if (!Profile.isFeatureEnabled(Profile.Feature.ROLLING_UPDATES)) {
    printFeatureDisabled();
    picocli.exit(CompatibilityResult.FEATURE_DISABLED);
    return;
}
```

`AbstractUpdatesCommand` already provides the shared `printFeatureDisabled()` helper and is the common superclass of both commands. A third subcommand added later would need to remember to copy this guard verbatim, or it would silently bypass the feature gate. Centralizing the guard (e.g., a `checkFeatureEnabled()` template method, or a shared `run()` template in the abstract base that calls a `doRun()` hook) would make the feature gate enforceable by construction rather than by convention.
